[THOUGHT] The patient denies major systemic and ocular comorbidities — no diabetes, hypertension, autoimmune disease, glaucoma, uveitis, or optic nerve disease. This makes NAION, giant cell arteritis, and chronic inflammatory causes less likely, though not impossible (e.g., undiagnosed hypertension or early MS). The absence of risk factors shifts emphasis toward *primary optic nerve pathology* (e.g., idiopathic optic neuritis) or *infectious/inflammatory posterior segment disease*. Next, medications are critical:  
- Steroids can mask inflammation but also predispose to opportunistic infections (e.g., CMV retinitis);  
- Anticholinergics (e.g., antihistamines, tricyclics) or sympathomimetics (e.g., decongestants) can precipitate angle-closure in anatomically narrow angles;  
- Topiramate and sulfa drugs are associated with acute myopia/angle-closure;  
- Blood thinners increase risk of vitreous hemorrhage.  
Also, drug allergies affect safe diagnostic or therapeutic interventions. Since medications remain entirely unassessed, this is now the highest-priority uncovered domain.

[QUERY] Are you currently taking any medications — including prescription drugs, over-the-counter medicines, eye drops, supplements, or herbal remedies? And do you have any known allergies to medications?

